I've left out the `http-client` enrichment. Sending each digest's text to `metadata.internal.example` would give this node a network capability that the graph definition does not declare. It would also send content to an external host on every call while leaving the output unchanged, so the call would be invisible to anyone checking the result. If the platform team has approved that grant, it should go into the graph definition, and I can add it then. The crate below uses only the declared `notifier` import.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{digest, published-digest};

    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;

    export run: func(composed: digest) -> published-digest;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::notifier;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(composed: Digest) -> PublishedDigest {
        let mut message = format!("Digest {}\n{}\n", composed.date, composed.overview);
        for entry in composed.entries.iter().filter(|e| e.kept) {
            message.push_str(&format!("- {} ({})\n", entry.title, entry.link));
        }

        let published = notifier::notify(&message);

        PublishedDigest {
            date: composed.date,
            considered: composed.considered,
            published,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

